A seat's workspace must start from origin when origin is ahead

`bin/session.mjs` preferred the **local** `advocate/<seat>` ref whenever one existed, falling
back to `origin/<seat>` only if there was no local branch at all. That is wrong in the one case
that actually happens on a machine running rounds: **something else pushed to the seat's
branch.**

A work order opened from CI is exactly that. It lands on `origin/advocate/<seat>` and never
touches the local ref. `round.sh` does `git fetch --all --prune`, which updates `origin/*` and
nothing else — so the next session starts from the stale local tip, never sees the order, writes
a session on top, and the push is rejected non-fast-forward.

**Nothing recovers from this on its own.** Every round afterwards repeats it and throws its own
work away, and the only trace is one line of `mechanical half failed` in the council log.

## What it has already cost, on this machine

Seven seats across three repositories, every one of them holding a remote-only commit dated
**2026-09-07** reading `<seat>: 1 commit(s) to read — work order open for a local session`:

| repository | seats | rounds lost | stranded locally |
|---|---|---|---|
| `NCCV-OPINION/cite-fort-collins` | `custody`, `contributor`, `one-operator` | 7, 7, 6 | quiet sessions |
| `FCCN-ANTIBODY/constellation.anecdote.channel` | `addressing`, `consent`, `presentation` | 6 each | `addressing` 5 drafts, `consent` 2 drafts |
| `Chaevity/ablative` | `console` | 7 | two sessions of 6 drafts each |

45 rejected pushes in `~/.advocate/council.log`. The content lost is mostly `quiet session` —
but the **work orders from 2026-09-07 have gone unread for six days**, which is the real harm:
the cloud side asked for a local session seven times and the local side could not hear it.

## The rule this puts in

When both refs exist, the tip that **contains** the other wins:

- remote contains local (behind, or identical) → start from `origin/<branch>`. The remote is the
  shared record.
- local contains remote (ahead) → start from the local branch. That is the ordinary state of a
  machine whose last push was disabled or offline, and its unpushed sessions must not be dropped.
- neither contains the other (genuinely diverged) → **refuse and say so.** It cannot be resolved
  here without discarding somebody's session, and one loud failure that names its own fix beats a
  silent one that repeats forever. `round.sh` already treats a nonzero exit from the mechanical
  half as a skipped seat, so this needs nothing on that side.

Only one ref, or neither: unchanged.

## The recovery it prints, and why it is four lines

The obvious advice — `git -C <root> rebase origin/<b> <b>` — **checks the seat branch out in the
root worktree and leaves it there**, parking the repository on an advocate branch. Several
appliances here commit on a schedule to whatever branch is checked out, so that is a real hazard
and not a tidiness point. The message uses a throwaway worktree instead.

## Verified

A sandbox repo (bare origin, a working clone, a third clone standing in for CI) driving
`bin/session.mjs` directly:

- **against `origin/main`'s copy** — the work order is *not* visible in the workspace and the
  session's push is rejected non-fast-forward. The bug, reproduced.
- **against this branch** — behind: the workspace contains `ORDER.md`. Ahead: the unpushed
  session survives. Diverged: exit 1 with the reconcile command.

`node --check` passes; `npm test` covers `yaml-enough`, `seats`, `adapter` and `petitions` and is
untouched by this.

## Not done here, and it needs a decision

**The seven diverged branches are still diverged.** This stops it happening again; it does not
reconcile what already happened. Each needs the four-line recovery run once, which pushes 6–9
local session commits to branches that have never seen them. That is a push to three
repositories' seat branches and I did not want to do it unasked — say the word and it is
mechanical.
